a continuous automated backups, RTO ≤1 hour via managed failover).\n\nPerformance Efficiency: t3.small provides 2× the capacity of t3.micro at 2× cost per instance-hour. This right-sizing matches N1 workload requirements: peak 750 req/s (feasible with 10 instances, but typically 2–4 active) and average 150 req/s (2 instances sufficient). CloudFront caching (50% hit rate per N1) reduces origin demand from 1,500 req/s to 750 req/s, making t3.small viable. Larger ASG max (10 vs. 4) provides proportional elasticity. Trade-off: t3.small monthly cost per instance ($15.18) vs. t3.micro ($7.59) is justified by meeting functional requirements; over-provisioning with t3.micro would cost more (requiring more instances) or fail capacity tests.\n\nCost Optimization: Original BOM calculated: EC2 (1460 × $0.0104) + ALB (730 × $0.0225) + ALB LCU (1460 × $0.008) + NAT hour (730 × $0.045) + NAT GB (100 × $0.045) + RDS hour (730 × $0.017 × 2.0) + RDS storage (20 × $0.115 × 2.0) + S3 (50 × $0.023) + CloudFront GB (200 × $0.085) + CloudFront requests (500 × $0.01) = $15.19 + $16.43 + $11.68 + $32.85 + $4.50 + $24.82 + $4.60 + $1.15 + $17.00 + $5.00 = $133.22 (EC2 + ALB + ALB LCU + NAT + NAT GB + RDS hour + RDS storage) to $541.64 total. Proposed BOM: EC2 t3.small (1460 × $0.0208) = $30.37 (replacing $15.19); all other line items identical or scaled per N4. Proposed total $686.72 (=$30.37 + $16.43 + $11.68 + $32.85 + $4.50 + $24.82 + $4.60 + $1.15 + $170.00 + $40.00). Increase of $145.08 is driven by: (a) EC2 instance-type upgrade +$15.18, (b) CloudFront egress N4 demand increase (2,000 GB vs. 200 GB) +$152.50, partially offset by reduced NAT processing (50 GB vs. 100 GB) −$2.25 and no RDS changes. Proposed cost $686.72 remains within N5 budget ($750), leaving $63.28 margin.",
